WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:01.200
Good morning, John.

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:03.469
Uh, he internet, is pretty great.

00:00:03.469 --> 00:00:06.460
It's weird and economically and culturally
diverse.

00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:09.680
It's my hobby and it's my job and it's my
passion - and it's my friend.

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:15.630
A lot of the marvelous, weirdness and diversity
of the internet was and is because of a concept

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:16.930
called Net Neutrality.

00:00:16.930 --> 00:00:21.030
Basically the idea that all the information
flowing through the pipes of the internet

00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:24.430
has to be treated the same way, which is kind
of a new thing.

00:00:24.430 --> 00:00:28.040
Other information distribution systems never
really had that rule which allowed for people

00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:30.170
who had money to come in and control everything.

00:00:30.170 --> 00:00:33.899
But, with the internet, whether you're watching,
like, the most popular television show on

00:00:33.899 --> 00:00:34.899
T.V.

00:00:34.899 --> 00:00:37.640
right now, or you're just watching me in my
office, talking to the camera and being a

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:41.909
weirdo, or you're looking up net neutrality
articles on Wikipedia, all of that information

00:00:41.909 --> 00:00:43.020
has to be treated the same.

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:46.450
The thing is, a lot of companies with a lot
of money, don't want it to be this way.

00:00:46.450 --> 00:00:51.030
They would rather say, "Well, if somebody
wants to pay us to deliver their content faster,

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:54.429
and slow everybody else down, then sure, yeah,
we want to do that."

00:00:54.429 --> 00:00:57.899
And some companies want to be able to pay
to speed up their content while competitors

00:00:57.899 --> 00:01:01.559
can't afford to do that, so that they can
maintain their strangle hold on the psyche

00:01:01.559 --> 00:01:02.559
of the American people.

00:01:02.559 --> 00:01:05.939
The people who want the internet to remain
neutral are mostly, people.

00:01:05.939 --> 00:01:11.200
We often feel like people, just normal people,
don't actually have that much influence on

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:13.369
how the American government is going to get
run.

00:01:13.369 --> 00:01:16.979
Now, with Net Neutrality, this is compounded
by the fact that both technologically and

00:01:16.979 --> 00:01:17.979
legally complected.

00:01:17.979 --> 00:01:21.729
So, it's kinda hard to get people on board
with the regulations, especially when pro-business

00:01:21.729 --> 00:01:25.150
people can just say, "Big government wants
to regulate the awesomest thing ever!"

00:01:25.150 --> 00:01:28.180
And that sounds a little scary; we don't want
the government to regulate the internet.

00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:29.369
I don't like the idea of that!

00:01:29.369 --> 00:01:33.110
But, we're not really talking about them regulating
the internet, we're talking about them regulating

00:01:33.110 --> 00:01:35.259
the companies that deliver the internet.

00:01:35.259 --> 00:01:39.060
So it's really come down to a vocal and active
group of people on the internet -- it's not

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:43.299
a very big group of people -- being the only
folks who are championing net neutrality.

00:01:43.299 --> 00:01:45.929
And, honestly, I didn't have a lot of hope
that we'd actually win.

00:01:45.929 --> 00:01:47.030
Like, not a big win.

00:01:47.030 --> 00:01:49.140
Maybe we'd force a middle course, you know?

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:53.119
But, look, Tom Wheeler of the FCC used to
work for the cable industry, and they hate

00:01:53.119 --> 00:01:54.119
net neutrality.

00:01:54.119 --> 00:01:56.590
And most individual people don't care about
net neutrality; they don't even know what

00:01:56.590 --> 00:01:57.590
net neutrality is.

00:01:57.590 --> 00:02:01.900
And practically all of the money being spent
by lobbyists is being spent to lobby for the

00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:03.680
destruction of net neutrality.

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:09.080
And yet, here I say it, in the United States
of America, where the internet has been reclassified

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:13.630
as a public utility, giving the government,
finally, the power to keep the internet neutral.

00:02:13.630 --> 00:02:17.530
And the new FCC rules guarantee,that they
will do that.

00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:18.720
We...won.

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:19.720
We won!

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:21.160
It's almost a foreign idea to me.

00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:22.200
But we won.

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:26.530
And it's a win against the lobbyists and against
corporate interests in a situation where there

00:02:26.530 --> 00:02:29.870
was no reason for the government to do the
right thing, except that it was the right

00:02:29.870 --> 00:02:30.870
thing.

00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:33.720
The other thing is that this really came down
to party lines, like there were three democrats

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:35.450
voting on the FCC and two republicans.

00:02:35.450 --> 00:02:37.350
And the republicans voted against net neutrality.

00:02:37.350 --> 00:02:41.570
So whenever people say, "It doesn't matter,
both sides are the same side," then something

00:02:41.570 --> 00:02:45.570
huge like this happens that would've totally
gone the other wa, if there had been a different

00:02:45.570 --> 00:02:46.811
administration in the White House.

00:02:46.811 --> 00:02:47.930
And it's huge!

00:02:47.930 --> 00:02:50.500
This is hugely important for the future of
the internet.

00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:52.510
It's hugely important for the future of me.

00:02:52.510 --> 00:02:56.400
But it was dragged out over a long time, and
it was complicated and it wasn't really sure

00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:58.810
when it was gonna actually happen and be done
with.

00:02:58.810 --> 00:03:01.571
So it's just not very exciting, but that's
how these things happen.

00:03:01.571 --> 00:03:06.060
And I would feel like a bit of a grapefruit
if I didn't say thank you to all of the people

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:11.290
who fought and worked hard to make the government
realize that they needed to keep the internet

00:03:11.290 --> 00:03:12.290
neutral.

00:03:12.290 --> 00:03:14.680
And also, to all of the people in the actual
government who decided to do this despite

00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:19.180
the fact that they're gonna make a lot of
corporations angry, and we know that those

00:03:19.180 --> 00:03:21.160
corporations have a lot of power in America.

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:25.590
And also, I would feel bad if i didn't just
take a little moment to celebrate and be happy

00:03:25.590 --> 00:03:29.670
about this thing that has finally happened
after a really long time of working on it.

00:03:29.670 --> 00:03:32.510
So, THANK YOU EVERYONE, THIS IS AWESOME!

00:03:32.510 --> 00:03:33.510
HUZZAH!!

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:34.860
John, I'll see you on Tuesday.

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:36.650
Oh look, Jimmy Carter's found his bed.

00:03:36.650 --> 00:03:37.650
Find a job!

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:41.130
John: Suddenly, you're just an old man playing
a video game with extremely out of date graphics.

00:03:41.130 --> 00:03:42.590
Hank: I'm just gonna get the coins and star.

00:03:42.590 --> 00:03:44.130
Katherine: You are such a nugget!

00:03:44.130 --> 00:03:49.580
Hank: This is the first time Bill Clinton
has EVER FLIRTED!

00:03:49.580 --> 00:03:51.510
Are you guys fighting over me right now?

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:52.900
Thank you Mario!

00:03:52.900 --> 00:03:56.140
But the princess is in another castle.

00:03:56.140 --> 00:03:57.610
John: Why does Toad talk like that?

00:03:57.610 --> 00:03:58.280
Hank: That's what he sounds like.

